# Where does every lesson show up in one game?
teaches: ["openings/principles","tactics/pin","calculation/combination"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How did Morphy's Opera Game (Paris, 1858) use development, pins and a sacrifice? Which of the course's habits would have saved Black?"
next: {"title":"Final test: how far have you come?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/30-final-test-how-far-have-you-come.cast"}

## Warm-up: a pin, then a guard
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2qk1nr/ppp1bppp/2n5/1B6/3P4/3Q1N2/PP3PPP/R1B2RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [d5]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/ppp1bppp/4pn2/6BQ/3P4/2NB1N2/PPP2PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6], arrows: [{from: h5, to: h7}, {from: d3, to: h7}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: one famous game, with nearly every lesson of this course inside it. First, two warm-up puzzles.
    draw position

B: Black's knight on c6 can't move. The bishop pins it to the king.
    highlight piece_b5 piece_c6

A: Day nine: a pinned piece can't run.
    highlight piece_d4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Attack the pinned knight with something cheap.","Your d-pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Pawn to d5 attacks the knight, and the knight can't step away: it's pinned to the king. Black loses a piece.
        wrong: Which of your pawns can attack the pinned knight?

B: A pawn against a knight that can't move.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Next one. White's queen and bishop both aim at h7.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

B: Queen takes h7, then?
    highlight piece_h7

A: Not yet: the knight on f6 guards h7.
    highlight piece_f6

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Remove the guard first.","Your bishop on g5."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a teaches ["tactics/removing-the-defender"]
        right: Bishop takes f6. If Black takes back, queen takes h7 is mate, so White stays a knight up.
        wrong: Before you take on h7, take away the piece that guards it.

B: So: pins, and guards. Where's the famous game?
    highlight piece_f6

A: A pin that wins, a guard pulled away, a mate on the back rank: one game in 1858 has them all.
    highlight piece_h5 piece_d3

## A night at the opera
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, d6, d4, Bg4, dxe5, Bxf3, Qxf3, dxe5, Bc4, Nf6, Qb3, Qe7, Nc3, c6, Bg5, b5, Nxb5, cxb5, Bxb5+, Nbd7, O-O-O, Rd8, Rxd7, Rxd7], arrows: [{from: f3, to: f7}, {from: c4, to: f7}, {from: b3, to: b7}, {from: g5, to: e7}, {from: b5, to: e8}, {from: d1, to: d7}, {from: b5, to: d7}]}
      - {fen: rn1qkbnr/ppp2ppp/3p4/4P3/4P1b1/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 4, moves: [dxe5, Qxd8+, Kxd8, Nxe5], arrows: [{from: e5, to: g4}, {from: e5, to: f7}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    counts:
      - b5
sources:
    - id: operawiki
      title: Opera Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Morphy v Duke Karl II of Brunswick and Count Isouard, played in a box at the Salle Ventadour, Paris, October or November 1858; 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 Bg4 … 17.Rd8# 1-0; Morphy could have won a pawn with 8.Qxb7; 9...b5? loses; 14...Qe6 tries to unpin the knight.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opera_Game
    - id: field1858
      title: Paul Morphy vs Duke Karl / Count Isouard, Paris 1858 (game score, from The Field, London, 4 December 1858)
      authors: Chessgames.com
      year: 2026
      finding: The same 33-ply score, 1-0, with Black a consultation pair.
      url: https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1233404
    - id: winter
      title: Paul Morphy (Chess Notes feature article)
      authors: Edward Winter
      year: 2026
      finding: The date (late October or early November 1858) and the opera performed are uncertain; the score is given as 1 e4 e5 … 17 Rd8 mate.
      url: https://chesshistory.com/winter/extra/morphy.html

    portrait morphy cites ["operawiki"] x 58 y 610 width 110 of "Paul Morphy" hidden true

    card title "A night at the opera"

A: Now the game. Paris, autumn 1858: Paul Morphy, a young American, sits in a box at the opera.
    draw position morphy

A: Against him, playing Black together, the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 2.4

    erase morphy

A: A knight to f3 and a pawn to d4: White takes the centre.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.2

B: Bishop to g4. I'd play that: it pins the knight to the queen.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_g4 piece_f3 piece_d1 duration 2

A: A pin, from day nine. But it doesn't hold the centre: White takes on e5.
    blocking false

    highlight piece_g4 piece_f3 duration 1.5

    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.2

A: If Black takes back, the queens come off, and the knight takes e5, hitting g4 and f7.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1

    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

    pause 0.5

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: Black would lose a pawn. So Black gives up the bishop for the knight instead.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 7 duration 0.4

    pause 0.6

    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.2

A: White's queen takes back, and comes out without losing a move.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 9 duration 2

    highlight piece_f3 duration 1.5

B: Black takes back on e5. Then bishop to c4: queen takes f7 would be mate.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 11 duration 1.2

    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: Black blocks the queen with a knight, and the queen jumps to b3: now b7 is attacked too.
    blocking false

    erase arrow_0

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 13 duration 1.2

    draw arrow_2

B: So Black's queen guards f7.
    animate plies_shown 14 duration 1.2

    erase arrow_1 arrow_2

A: Morphy could grab b7, but a queen check from b4 would force the queens off. He brings out another piece instead.
    blocking false

    highlight piece_b7 duration 2.2

    animate plies_shown 15 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_c3 duration 1.5

A: Black plays c6, and bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6 to the queen: a second pin.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 16 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 17 duration 1.2

    draw arrow_3

    erase arrow_3

A: Count: four White pieces out, two for Black, and Black's king still on e8.
    highlight piece_e8 piece_f6 piece_e7

B: Then I'd chase the bishop: pawn to b5!
    highlight piece_b7

A: That's what Black played. How many white pieces attack that pawn?
    animate plies_shown 18 duration 1.2

    pause 0.3

    quiz "Black's pawn on b5: how many white pieces attack it?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 920 buttons_at.y 380 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Three: the knight, the bishop, and the queen down the b-file. Only the c6 pawn defends.
        wrong: Count down files and diagonals too.
        * One
        * Two
        + Three
        * Four

    draw count_b5

    pause 0.6

    erase count_b5

A: And Morphy takes with the knight: a knight for a pawn, to open lines to a king still in the centre.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 19 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_e8 duration 3

A: Black takes back, a knight up. Bishop takes b5, check: two pawns for the knight, and the lines are open.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 20 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 21 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_e8 duration 2

A: Black blocks with the other knight, and now it's pinned to the king: it can't move at all.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 22 duration 1.2

    draw arrow_4

    highlight piece_d7 duration 2

A: Castles long: the king is safe, and a rook joins the attack on the pinned knight.
    blocking false

    erase arrow_4

    animate plies_shown 23 duration 1.6

    draw arrow_5 arrow_6 parallel true

A: Black guards it with the rook. Rook takes d7 anyway: a rook for a knight, because the rook that takes back is pinned too.
    blocking false

    erase arrow_5 arrow_6

    animate plies_shown 24 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 25 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 26 duration 1.2

    draw arrow_4

    pause 0.5

    erase arrow_4

A: So which habits would have saved Black?
    highlight piece_e8

    pause 0.5

A: Develop and castle, from day five: Black's king never left e8. And before b5, the Three Questions: what can White take after it?
    highlight piece_e8 piece_f8 piece_h8

## Your turn: finish it like Morphy
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r5k1/4qpp1/1p1p3p/4p3/1PP1P3/3K3Q/5PPP/3R4 b - - 0 1, moves: [Ra3+, Ke2, Rxh3], flip: true}
      - {fen: 4kb1r/p2rqppp/5n2/1B2p1B1/4P3/1Q6/PPP2PPP/2K4R w k - 0 14, moves: [Rd1, Qe6, Bxd7+, Nxd7, Qb8+, Nxb8, Rd8#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: operawiki
      title: Opera Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 14...Qe6 is a futile attempt to unpin the knight; 15.Bxd7+ Nxd7 16.Qb8+! Nxb8 17.Rd8#.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opera_Game
    - id: field1858
      title: Paul Morphy vs Duke Karl / Count Isouard, Paris 1858 (game score, from The Field, London, 4 December 1858)
      authors: Chessgames.com
      year: 2026
      finding: The game's score, 1-0 in 17 moves.
      url: https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1233404
    - id: operamate
      title: Opera mate (1 move)
      authors: chesspuzzle.net
      year: 2026
      finding: A rook mates next to the king, protected by a bishop that also covers the escape square; named after Morphy's Opera Game of 1858.
      url: https://chesspuzzle.net/Learn/OperaMate

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you've seen how Morphy got there: your turn. First, a puzzle from Black's side, so the board turns round.
    draw position

B: White's king has walked out to d3.
    highlight piece_d3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A check along a line.","Your rook on a8."] themes ["skewer"] voice a teaches ["tactics/skewer"]
        right: Rook to a3, check: the king has to step off the rank, and the rook takes the queen behind it. A skewer, from day nine.
        wrong: Which check hits the king with something bigger behind it?

A: Back to the opera, after move thirteen, and back to White's side. Three of Morphy's moves, scored.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    highlight piece_b5 piece_d7 piece_e8 duration 3.5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find Morphy's move." widget chess line 1 themes ["pin"] voice a test opera
        right: Rook to d1: the pinned rook on d7 is attacked twice now, and it can't move.

A: Black's queen goes to e6, to free the knight on f6 from its pin.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find Morphy's move." widget chess line 1 themes ["pin","removing the defender"] voice a test opera teaches ["tactics/pin","tactics/removing-the-defender"]
        right: Bishop takes d7, check: the pinned rook falls.

B: The knight took back. Wouldn't the queen take?
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.6

A: The queen would have lasted longer, but Black was lost either way.
    highlight piece_e6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line 3 themes ["deflection","mating pattern","combination"] voice a test opera teaches ["tactics/deflection","tactics/mating-patterns","calculation/combination"]
        right: Queen to b8, check! The knight has to take, and rook to d8 is mate: the bishop on g5 guards the rook and the square e7.

    pause 0.6

    erase position

    text score_big "Opera score" x 500 y 400 font_size 110

A: You scored {opera.score} of {opera.total}.
    results test opera

    pause 1

    erase score_big

    draw position

A: The pins came from day nine; pulling the knight off the d-file, from day ten.
    highlight piece_b8

B: And the rook and bishop mate is from day eleven.
    highlight piece_d8 piece_g5

A: It's called the Opera mate, after this game. All of it in a row is a combination, day thirteen.
    highlight piece_e8

A: Morphy needed no magic: he developed, pinned and piled up, and the sacrifice only cashed in.
    highlight piece_g5

    pause 0.5

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 440 font_size 52

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_mistake "its first mistake" x 500 y 350 font_size 52

